
#  Arrays

1. Basics
  // Declaration + creation
  int[] arr = new int[5];              // default values: 0
  int[] arr2 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};        // literal init
  int[] arr3 = new int[]{1, 2, 3};     // explicit new + literal

  // 2D array
  int[][] grid = new int[3][4];        // 3 rows, 4 cols
  int[][] grid2 = {{1,2},{3,4},{5,6}}; // jagged-capable

  // Length
  arr.length;        // NOT arr.length() — that's the #1 mistake
  grid.length;       // number of rows
  grid[0].length;    // number of cols in row 0

  // Traversal
  for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) { }
  for (int x : arr) { }                // enhanced for-loop (read-only copy)

  // Common utility methods (java.util.Arrays)
  Arrays.sort(arr);                              // ascending, in-place
  Arrays.sort(arr, Collections.reverseOrder());  // only works on Integer[], not int[]
  Arrays.fill(arr, 0);                           // fill all with value
  Arrays.copyOf(arr, newLength);                 // resize (pads with 0)
  Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, from, to);              // [from, to)
  Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2);                     // element-wise equality
  Arrays.toString(arr);                          // for printing
  Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3);                        // fixed-size List view (careful!)
  int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, key);       // array MUST be sorted first

  Key gotchas (interviewers love these):

  - int[] is primitive → sorting descending needs a trick (see below) since Collections.reverseOrder() needs Integer[].
  - arr.clone() does a shallow copy for 2D arrays — inner arrays are still shared references.
  - Arrays are fixed size — no add/remove. That's the whole reason ArrayList exists.
  - Default values: 0 for numeric, false for boolean, null for objects.

  Descending sort trick for primitive int[]:

 // Box manually, sort descending, then unbox back — no streams needed
 Integer[] boxed = new Integer[arr.length];
 for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) boxed[i] = arr[i];
 Arrays.sort(boxed, Collections.reverseOrder());
 for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) arr[i] = boxed[i];

2. Easy — Foundational Patterns
   - Pattern: Two Pointers (opposite ends)

     Used for: reverse array, palindrome check, pair-sum in sorted array.

     java
     void reverse(int[] arr) {
         int l = 0, r = arr.length - 1;
         while (l < r) {
             int tmp = arr[l]; arr[l] = arr[r]; arr[r] = tmp;
             l++; r--;
         }
     }

